{
  "dom_id": "step-q39351",
  "name": "Question 1",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/d953b897affdad991b86db265b90610cb6e28f3ac6a94889c5143f2289d93f68.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is shown above. Find the values ​​of $a$ such that $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to a.$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/linear/14227291/assets/e9862ea9f1debb162a62a3eb3422f2313f4a8512c30fe2a16e982207639c0ef4.png)\n\nFrom the given graph, we see that as $x$ approaches $-3$ or $2$, the values of $f(x)$ increase, approaching $\\infty .$ \n\nSo, we can write: $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to -3$ and $f(x)\\to \\infty $ as $x\\to 2.$ \n\nTherefore, $a=-3$ and $a=2.$",
